 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:10:15.000553Z",
 "text": "No, the clause requiring a refundable pet deposit is not enforceable. \n\nUnder the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006*, any money collected by a landlord to be held against potential liability (such as property damage) or to be returned upon a condition at the end of a tenancy meets the statutory definition of a \"security deposit.\" **Section 105** strictly prohibits landlords from requiring or receiving any security deposit other than a lawful rent deposit (typically used for the last month's rent under section 106). Because damage deposits—including refundable pet deposits—are prohibited by section 105, this requirement is inconsistent with the Act and void. (While the portion welcoming pets is permissible, the deposit requirement itself cannot be enforced.)\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 105",
